\id 2PE - The English New Testament According to Family 35
\h 2 Peter
\toc3 2 Pet.
\toc2 2 Peter
\toc1 The Second Epistle of Peter
\mt2 The Second Epistle of
\mt1 PETER
\c 1
\s1 To fellow believers
\m
\v 1 Simon Peter, slave\f + \fr 1:1 \ft That is right, “slave”—Jude (v. 1), James (1:1) and Paul (Romans 1:1) also call themselves slaves of Jesus Christ.\f* and apostle of Jesus Christ, to those who have obtained\f + \fr 1:1 \ft The verb here suggests receiving something by lot or appointment; in this case the source would be God's will: this faith is something God apportioned to them.\f* with us the same kind of faith in the righteousness\f + \fr 1:1 \ft That is right, faith in Christ's righteousness: 1) that Jesus was righteous, not dying for any wrongdoing of His own; 2) that being intrinsically righteous He can impart His righteousness to us.\f* of our God and Savior,\f + \fr 1:1 \ft “God and Savior” refer to a single person, the Son.\f* Jesus Christ:
\v 2 may grace and peace be multiplied to you\f + \fr 1:2 \ft “Multiplied”—not just a little, lots. In his first letter Peter ended the salutation here, but now he fleshes out the means.\f* through a real knowledge of God\f + \fr 1:2 \ft As our genuine knowledge of God grows, so the amount or degree of grace and peace we receive also grows.\f* and of our Lord Jesus,\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Here the reference is to two persons, Father and Son—although the grammar could be taken to indicate a single person, in which case I would render ‘of God, even our Lord Jesus’ (or ‘Jesus our Lord’). But looking at the next verse, “His divine power” refers to the Father, because “the One who called” refers to the Son.\f*
\v 3 in that His divine power has granted to us all things \it pertaining\it* to life and godliness, through the real knowledge of the One\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Again, we only appropriate the complete provision for “life and godliness” to the degree that we grow in our genuine knowledge of God. As Creator He made everything upon which life depends, including life itself, but our understanding of and appreciation for His provision is measured by our relationship with Him.\f* who called us by glory and excellence,\f + \fr 1:3 \ft I take it that Peter is saying that it is the glory and moral excellence of Christ that attracts us, but there is the extraordinary promise that we can share in those qualities, as he spells out in verse 4. Instead of “by glory”, perhaps 19% of the Greek manuscripts have ‘by His own glory’ (as in NIV, NASB, LB, TEV, etc.).\f*
\v 4 through which\f + \fr 1:4 \ft “Which” is plural and presumably refers back to “glory and excellence”.\f* He has granted to us such precious and extraordinary promises, so that through these\f + \fr 1:4 \ft The promises—but of course we have to appropriate them.\f* you may become partakers of a divine nature,\f + \fr 1:4 \ft There is no definite article with “divine nature”; “become” indicates a process—the more like Christ we become, the more divine will our nature be.\f* having escaped the depravity that is in the world because of lust.\f + \fr 1:4 \ft At times Peter's syntax seems to be almost as convoluted as Paul's; the first four verses form a single sentence. The precise interrelationship of the dependent clauses is not transparent. The notes above give my understanding of Peter's intent. It is the inordinate desire (lust) for anything in this world that leads to depravity. The proper amount of food, drink, pleasure, money, power or whatever is a good thing. It is when people want more that they get into trouble.\f*
\ms1 Take the Truth seriously!
\s1 Our ‘entrance’ depends on us
\p
\v 5 So for this very reason, making every effort,\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Here Peter emphasizes human responsibility, heavily.\f* to your faith add excellence,\f + \fr 1:5 \ft The word used here can refer to excellence in any area, but in this context the emphasis is presumably on moral excellence.\f* and to the excellence knowledge,
\v 6 and to the knowledge self-control, and to the self-control perseverance, and to the perseverance godliness,
\v 7 and to the godliness brotherly affection, and to the brotherly affection love.\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Do these qualities have to be added in this sequence, or can we tackle them in any order, or even all at once? If this sequence must be followed I do not understand how it works. I believe spiritual growth moves in an upward spiral; the Holy Spirit makes the rounds of all areas, progressively increasing the level of excellence, or Christ-likeness. I take this to be the point of the “increasing” in verse 8.\f*
\v 8 Because with these things existing and increasing in you, it makes \it you to be\it* neither useless nor unfruitful in the real knowledge\f + \fr 1:8 \ft I have used “real knowledge” repeatedly because I take that to be the intent of the Text. Real knowledge of God contrasts with human ideas about God and false ‘knowledge’ furnished by demonic prophecies, visions, etc. It also contrasts with \+nd true\+nd* theoretical knowledge about God—\+nd real\+nd* knowledge must be experiential, as well as being true. Notice that we are supposed to be useful, to be fruitful. If we do not grow, something is definitely wrong, as verse 9 makes clear.\f* of our Lord Jesus Christ.
\v 9 For he who lacks these things is blind, being \it so\it* myopic, having forgotten the cleansing from his past sins.\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Peter says that someone who lacks the qualities listed in verses 5-7 has forgotten his cleansing and become myopic to the point of being practically blind. If we do not walk with God, do not study His Word, do not listen to the Holy Spirit, Satan will cheerfully take care of the rest.\f*
\p
\v 10 Therefore, brothers, you should be all the more diligent to make your calling and election sure,\f + \fr 1:10 \ft As in his first letter, Peter emphasizes both divine sovereignty and human responsibility, only this verse is especially bothersome to certain theological systems. If we can make our election sure, then we can also make it unsure, or words have no meaning. Since both truths are clearly presented from cover to cover of the Bible, theological systems that emphasize only one of the two do not do justice to the Sacred Text. To emphasize only one is to hop on one foot—to walk, run and work efficiently you really need both feet. To our limited human logic the two truths appear to be incompatible, but to God's infinite logic they obviously are not. Those who are wise will bow to God's logic.\f* for if you do these things you will never stumble.\f + \fr 1:10 \ft The verb used is ‘to stumble’, not ‘to fall’, but the tense used suggests a permanent consequence.\f*
\v 11 Because in this way you will be granted the abundant entrance\f + \fr 1:11 \ft The Text has the definite article; I take the point to be that there will be different levels or kinds of “entrance”; some will have many rewards, others some, others none (1 Corinthians 3:15). Those who follow the orientation in these verses will receive many rewards, they will get “the abundant entrance”. (I suppose we will all agree that it is better to get in by the skin of your teeth [1 Corinthians 3:15] than not to get in at all, but would it not be better yet to have some rewards? The amount is up to us.)\f* into the unending kingdom of our Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ.\f + \fr 1:11 \ft To participate in that “unending Kingdom” is surely worth whatever effort is required of us. The difficulty is that all such effort must be expended in faith.\f*
\s1 Peter's death is imminent
\p
\v 12 So then, I will not neglect to keep reminding you about these things, though you know and are established in the present truth.
\v 13 Yes, I think it right to stir you up by a reminder, as long as I am in this ‘tent’,
\v 14 knowing that the shedding of my ‘tent’ is imminent, just as our Lord Jesus Christ has made clear to me.\f + \fr 1:14 \ft The Lord told Peter his time was up, and evidently did the same for Paul (2 Timothy 4:6). “All the days ordained for me were written in your book before one of them came to be” (Psalm 139:16). A servant of God dies only at the appointed time.\f*
\v 15 Moreover I will take pains to provide a reminder of these things for you to have,\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Verses 12 & 13 refer to repeated reminders while he is still in his ‘tent’, which would be his own ongoing activity; so why the ‘moreover’ in verse 15? In the NKJV verse 15 reads: “Moreover, I will be careful to ensure that you always have a reminder of these things after my decease”. Well, how can you ‘ensure’ that someone will ‘always have a reminder’ of something? It seems clear to me that the something has to be written down; a reminder has to be in writing, to be guaranteed. So what is Peter's intention? He specifies “a reminder of these things”, so what are the ‘these things’? They are evidently the things he will discuss in this letter. But he must be referring to something more than the initial draft of the letter (or the verse becomes meaningless)—hence, multiple copies. For more on the subject of multiple copies, please see the article “Multiple Autograph Copies”, available from my website: www.prunch.org.\f* as needed, after my exit.
\s1 The prophetic Word is trustworthy
\p
\v 16 Now then, we did not follow craftily devised fables when we made known to you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ; rather we were attentive eyewitnesses of His majesty
\v 17 —because He received from God the Father honor and glory when that \bd Voice\bd*\f + \fr 1:17 \ft Peter never forgot that VOICE; he even coined the phrase, Magnificent Glory, to describe the source. Evidently what happened on that mountain was totally overwhelming. What happened there turned an ordinary mountain into a “holy” one.\f* was directed to Him by the Magnificent Glory, “This is my Son, the beloved, in whom I am \it always\it* well pleased”—
\v 18 yes, we heard this Voice being uttered from heaven when we were with Him on the holy mountain.\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Verses 16-18 have to do with the transfiguration experience: those present had a foretaste of “the power and coming” (verse 16) of the Lord; they experienced a microcosm of the Messianic Millennial Kingdom.\f*
\p
\v 19 And so we have the most reliable prophetic Word,\f + \fr 1:19 \ft “Reliable prophetic Word” about what? About the Messianic Millennial Kingdom—the mount of transfiguration event confirmed all the OT prophecies that there would be such a Kingdom.\f* to which you do well to pay attention as to a lamp shining in a dark place,\f + \fr 1:19 \ft In this dark world we need the light of God's Word, but when all prophecies are fulfilled and we are glorified, when we are basking in the light of the Lamb (Revelation 21:23), the prophetic Word we now know will belong to ancient history.\f* until the day dawns and the morning star rises in your hearts;\f + \fr 1:19 \ft An interesting figure of speech—I imagine that “the day dawning” and “the morning star rising” are basically synonymous, since the morning star heralds the new day. But the new day happens in our hearts; eternal life is a quality of life that we can start enjoying right now, in this world. However, Peter's use of “until” points to the future Glorious Day.\f*
\v 20 knowing this first, that no Prophecy of Scripture comes to be from private release;\f + \fr 1:20 \ft The word rendered “release” occurs only here in the New Testament, but the basic meaning of the root is ‘to loose’ or ‘release’. With reference to a prophetic word it could refer either to its enunciation/origination or to its interpretation. Verse 21 makes clear that here it is the origination. False or fake prophecies derive from the will of the ‘prophet’ (or demonic influence), but true prophecy never does.\f*
\v 21 for no Prophecy ever came by the will of man, rather holy men of God spoke as they were carried along by the Holy Spirit.\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Here we have an impressive description of the process of Inspiration.\f*
\c 2
\ms1 Watch out for false teachers!
\s1 Their teachings are destructive
\m
\v 1 However, there were also \nd false\nd* prophets among the people, just as, indeed, there will be false teachers among you, who will introduce\f + \fr 2:1 \ft The verb here does not normally imply secrecy; purveyors of false ideas usually come on as sincere, above board, confident, even bold.\f* destructive heresies, even denying the Owner who bought them (bringing on themselves swift destruction).\f + \fr 2:1 \ft Sometimes we wish that destruction were a little ‘swifter’, but 3:9 below gives the answer.\f*
\v 2 And many will follow their licentious ways, because of which the way of the Truth will be defamed.\f + \fr 2:2 \ft The biblical worldview is defamed.\f*
\v 3 And in covetousness they will exploit you with deceptive words; since way back the judgment for such people is not slack, and their destruction will not sleep.
\s1 Their doom is sure
\p
\v 4 Because if God did not spare angels when they sinned, but delivered them into chains of darkness, confining them in Tartarus\f + \fr 2:4 \ft Tartarus is not to be equated with Hades (the holding area for departed human spirits) nor with the Lake of Fire, since the Text here clearly says those angels are being \+nd reserved\+nd* for judgment. The day when Satan and his angels will be cast into the Lake is still future (Revelation 20:10, Matthew 25:41). So Tartarus is a prison for certain fallen angels—for more about those angels see 1 Peter 3:19-20 and Jude 6-7.\f* to be reserved for judgment,
\v 5 and did not spare the ancient world but preserved Noah, with seven others, a preacher of righteousness, when He brought a flood on the world of the ungodly,
\v 6 and condemned the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah to destruction, reducing them to ashes (providing a warning to those who are intent on being ungodly),\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Unfortunately, “those who are intent on being ungodly” could care less about any such warnings, even if they have any awareness of them.\f*
\v 7 while He rescued upright Lot, who was distressed by the licentious lifestyle of the wicked
\v 8 (for that upright man,\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Compared to the people around him Lot was definitely “upright”, but evidently it was mostly a passive uprightness; he did not make any converts, and even lost his immediate family.\f* dwelling among them, tormented an upright soul from day to day by seeing and hearing lawless deeds)
\v 9 —\it then\it* the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of testings and to reserve the unjust under punishment\f + \fr 2:9 \ft They are under punishment already \+nd before\+nd* the Day of judgment. Compare Luke 16:19-31, which is \+nd not\+nd* said to be a parable: in Hades (which is not the Lake of Fire), before the final judgment, the rich man was already being punished (when someone is put in prison he is already being punished, before his case is tried and sentence pronounced). In Hades there is a great gulf between the just and the unjust. Since we determine our destination by what we do in this life, and there is no second chance (the rich man knew he was condemned), the punishment, or the blessing, starts at physical death.\f* for the day of judgment,
\v 10 and especially those who go after the flesh in the lust for defilement,\f + \fr 2:10 \ft People who are greedy in their moral depravity tend not to respect (or even acknowledge) authority, including that of angelic beings.\f* and despise authority.
\s1 Their depravity is ‘unreal’
\p They are audacious, self-willed; they are not afraid to revile majestic beings,\f + \fr 2:10 \ft Literally ‘glories’—based on the next verse, it appears that they are high ranking fallen angels.\f*
\v 11 whereas angels, though being greater in might and power,\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Greater than unregenerate human beings (presumably not than those seated in Christ—Ephesians 1:20-21, 2:6).\f* do not bring a reviling accusation against them before the Lord.\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Compare Jude 9.\f*
\v 12 But these, as unreasoning animals, creatures of instinct made to be caught and destroyed, spouting off in matters of which they are ignorant, will be destroyed by their own depravity,\f + \fr 2:12 \ft Depravity destroys the quality of life already, down here, even before death and judgment.\f*
\v 13 receiving the wages of unrighteousness. Counting it a pleasure to carouse in the daytime, they are stains and blemishes, reveling in their own deceptions\f + \fr 2:13 \ft “Reveling in their own deceptions”—I imagine all of us know people like this.\f* while they feast with you,
\v 14 having eyes full of adultery and that cannot cease from sin,\f + \fr 2:14 \ft The eyes don't stop sinning. I am reminded of our Lord's words in Matthew 6:22-23. “The lamp of the body is the eye. So if your eye is sound your whole body will be full of light. But if your eye is evil your whole body will be full of darkness. So if the light that is in you is darkness, how great is that darkness!” Of course we have two eyes, but the Text has ‘eye’ in the singular. I take it that the reference is to the way we interpret what we see (which is our real ‘eye’)—two people, one pure and one vile, observing the same scene will give very different interpretations to it. ‘Evil’ here has the idea of malignant, aggressively evil. Someone with a malignant mind will give an evil interpretation to \+nd everything\+nd* he sees, and in consequence his being will be filled with unrelenting darkness. Compare Titus 1:15.\f* enticing unstable souls; having a heart trained in greediness they are children of a curse;\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Peter, a Jew, uses a Jewish idiom—they are characterized by curse. They are a walking curse, damaging everyone they touch.\f*
\v 15 having forsaken the right way\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Their first decision was to forsake the right—once you turn out the light you condemn yourself to stumble around in the dark.\f* they have gone astray, having followed the way of Balaam of Bosor, who loved the wages of unrighteousness;
\v 16 but he got a rebuke for his wrongdoing—a mute donkey speaking with a human voice restrained the insanity of the prophet.\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Peter says that what Balaam did was ‘insane’, just like the mercenary ‘prophets’ of our day.\f*
\v 17 These are waterless springs, clouds driven by a storm,\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Such clouds often rush past without dropping any water. The false teachers make all sorts of promises, but being empty they cannot deliver; they cheat their hearers.\f* for whom the blackest of the darkness\f + \fr 2:17 \ft This darkness is associated with Satan's kingdom. Peter is saying that they will share Satan's destiny. The description given to these false teachers is unusually severe, Peter really does not like them! Since God hates those who have sold themselves to do evil (Psalm 5:5-6, Deuteronomy 7:10), so does Peter, and so must we. (Psalm 97:10 has a \+nd command\+nd*: “You who love Jehovah, hate evil!” David, at least, obeyed it—Psalm 26:5, 31:6, 119:113, 139:21-22. How about us?) God's love includes hating evil, of necessity, because of the consequences of the evil. If God loves us He must be against whatever may harm us.\f* has been reserved forever.\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Just over 2% of the Greek manuscripts, of objectively inferior quality, omit “forever” (as in NIV, NASB, LB, TEV, etc.).\f*
\s1 Their victims lose out
\p
\v 18 Because speaking pompous words of emptiness they entice through fleshly lusts, through licentiousness, those who have actually escaped from those who are living in error,
\v 19 promising them freedom while they themselves are slaves of corruption; for by whom a person is overcome, by him also he is brought into bondage.
\v 20 For if, after they have escaped the defilements of the world through the real knowledge of the Lord and Savior Jesus Christ, they are again entangled in them and overcome, the latter circumstances have become worse for them than the first ones.
\v 21 For it would have been better\f + \fr 2:21 \ft Oh my, batten down the hatches! If it would have been \+bd better\+bd* for them not to have known, then of necessity they are now worse off than they were before they knew (as verse 20 makes clear). But before they knew they were lost, so what can be worse than that? To be lost without a chance. Before, they were lost but had the option to be saved. But once they know the Truth, if they throw it away there is no second chance. Hebrews 6:4-6 is plain enough (if you hear someone twist the Text into a pretzel in an effort to avoid the plain meaning, you may be sure that whatever position he is trying to defend is incorrect). “For it is impossible for those who were once for all [\+it hapax\+it*] enlightened, and have tasted the heavenly gift, and have become sharers in the Holy Spirit, and have tasted the good Word of God and the powers of the coming age, and have fallen away—\+it it is impossible\+it* to renew them again to repentance, thereby crucifying the Son of God \+bd again\+bd*, \+it just\+it* for themselves, holding Him up to contempt.” You cannot crucify \+nd again\+nd* unless you have crucified at least once already. For someone to crucify Jesus \+nd again\+nd* must mean that he has indeed taken advantage of the first crucifixion, he was indeed saved—if he was never really saved he never really appropriated the first crucifixion, so he cannot do it \+bd again\+bd*.
\fp I have used ‘to really know’ to translate the heightened form επιγινωσκω (in contrast to γινωσκω, ‘to know’) and ‘real knowledge’ to translate the heightened form επιγνωσις (in contrast to γνωσις, ‘knowledge’). I consider that the Holy Spirit is an intelligent and honest user of human language, so His choice of the heightened forms was deliberate. Those who seek to arrive at the intended meaning of the Text need to respect that deliberate choice. When Peter says someone has a “real knowledge of the Lord and Savior Jesus Christ”, that someone must be saved.
\fp To elevate a doctrinal system above the Word of God is a form of idolatry.\f* for them not to have really known the way of the righteousness, than having really known it to turn back from the holy commandment delivered to them.
\v 22 But it has happened to them according to the true proverb: a dog returns to its own vomit and a bathed sow to wallowing in mud.
\c 3
\ms1 So take the Truth seriously!!
\m
\v 1 Dear ones, this is now a second letter I am writing to you\f + \fr 3:1 \ft See 1 Peter 1:1 for the identification of the addressees.\f* (in both of which I stir up your sincere mind by way of reminder),
\v 2 so that you remember the words that were spoken before by the holy prophets and the command of the Lord and Savior, through your apostles.\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Peter places the “command of the Lord and Savior through your apostles” (which I take to be a reference to NT writings) on a par with the “words spoken before by the holy prophets”, which evidently refers to OT writings. Since “apostles” is plural, I take it that Peter was aware that there were several authors.\f*
\s1 Mockers will come
\p
\v 3 Knowing this first: in the last days mockers will come, living according to their own lusts
\v 4 and saying: “Where is the promise of his coming? For since the fathers fell asleep all things continue as they were from the beginning of creation.”
\v 5 This because they deliberately ignore\f + \fr 3:5 \ft It appears to me that the term “deliberately” has a direct bearing on the intended meaning. How can one ‘forget’ deliberately? To ‘ignore’ is deliberate; to ‘pretend’ is deliberate. When a professor or a scholar or a scientist ignores the scientific evidence for a worldwide flood he is deliberately deceiving his students or readers. To do so is to be perverse.\f* that heavens\f + \fr 3:5 \ft There is no definite article with ‘heavens’, I suppose because the third heaven, God's abode, is not included—the reference here would be to the first heaven (the atmosphere) and the second (stellar space).\f* and land (out of water and through water)\f + \fr 3:5 \ft To be visible and useable (for normal purposes) land must rise above the water level, ‘out of water’, but to sustain life there must be water available on the land as well, ‘through/by water’.\f* had been existing from of old by the word of God,
\v 6 by which \it word\it* the world\f + \fr 3:6 \ft “World” is different from “land” (v. 5) and refers to the organized world—Noah's flood did not destroy the planet earth, just the organized surface. It was the divine decree that unleashed the destructive forces.\f* that then was perished, being flooded by water.\f + \fr 3:6 \ft All around the world there are layers of sedimentary material hundreds of meters deep, and the layerings are similar. The whole globe was scoured and the surface rearranged about 4350 years ago. To ignore the evidence is a deliberate choice, and has serious consequences.\f*
\s1 This world will be destroyed
\p
\v 7 But the current heavens, and the earth, are being reserved by His word for fire,\f + \fr 3:7 \ft God promised Noah that He would never destroy the world by water again, designating the rainbow as a symbol of that promise (there were no rainbows before the Flood)—the next time it will be by fire. The destruction in Noah's day involved the earth and the first heaven, the atmosphere; the next destruction will include the second heaven, stellar space, as well.\f* being preserved until the day of judgment and perdition of godless men.\f + \fr 3:7 \ft “Preserved”—our planet was created for the use of the human race and placed under human administration, so it cannot be destroyed until human history has run its course.\f*
\s2 The Lord is longsuffering
\p
\v 8 But dear ones, do not forget this one thing, that with the Lord one day is as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day.\f + \fr 3:8 \ft Although I myself have been partial to the idea that this information constitutes a precise formula, it is doubtless more prudent to take this as a general statement, showing that an eternal Being does not regard time the way we do.\f*
\v 9 The Lord is not delaying the promise, as some consider delay, but rather is patient toward us, not wishing anyone to perish but all to come into repentance.\f + \fr 3:9 \ft If everything were ‘cut and dried’, there would be no point in waiting.\f*
\s2 Hasten the Day of God
\p
\v 10 However, the day of the Lord will come like a thief at night, in which heavens\f + \fr 3:10 \ft The same comment applies as in verse 5 above.\f* will pass away with a whoosh, while the elements will be dissolved by burning—both the earth and the works in it will be completely consumed.
\v 11 Therefore, since all these things will be destroyed, what kind of people ought you to be in holy living and godliness,
\v 12 expecting and hastening\f + \fr 3:12 \ft “Hastening” is a transitive verb. If we can hasten the coming, we can also delay it, or words do not mean anything. The point is, what we do or do not do \+nd makes a difference\+nd*, which is why there will be an accounting. I imagine that there are outside limits or parameters that God's sovereignty will guarantee, but within those parameters it is up to us (like players on a football field—the score is up to them). So how can we hasten the coming of God's day? By living our lives to the hilt on behalf of His Kingdom, standing up for biblical values in the society at large, working to get the Gospel to the last ethnic nation.\f* the coming of the day of God, because of which heavens, being on fire, will be dissolved, and the elements will melt with fervent heat?
\s2 A better world is coming
\p
\v 13 Nevertheless, we look for new heavens and a new earth\f + \fr 3:13 \ft God is not through creating, but since the Son will have His bride, the next time around righteousness will be guaranteed.\f* in which dwells righteousness, according to His promise.
\s1 So be serious!
\p
\v 14 Therefore, dear ones, looking forward to these things, be diligent to be found by Him in peace,\f + \fr 3:14 \ft When we face the Creator are we going to see a smile, or a frown—it is up to us.\f* faultless and blameless;
\v 15 and consider the longsuffering of our Lord to be salvation\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Peter repeats the truth stated in verse 9 above—just because God is not striking us down like He did with Ananias and Sapphira (Acts 5:1-11) does not mean that we are getting a free ride. If you have sense, you look ahead.\f*—just as our beloved brother Paul also wrote to you, according to the wisdom given to him,
\v 16 as also in all his epistles\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Peter was obviously aware of a ‘Pauline corpus’—the use of ‘all’ suggests that Peter was aware of a fair number of Paul's letters.\f* (speaking in them of these things, about which there are some things hard to understand), which the untaught and unstable distort, as they do also the rest of the Scriptures,\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Peter affirms that Paul's letters are Scripture!\f* to their own destruction.\f + \fr 3:16 \ft The verb form of this noun is usually rendered ‘to perish’—the implication is that such people are lost. To distort what is in a newspaper or magazine does not have the same consequence—it is distorting God's Word that is serious.\f*
\v 17 You therefore, dear ones, being forewarned, be on guard lest, being carried away by the error of the wicked, you fall from your own steadfastness.\f + \fr 3:17 \ft Peter emphasizes human responsibility, again.\f*
\v 18 Rather, may you grow in grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ.
\p To Him be the glory both now and into the eternal day.\f + \fr 3:18 \ft Peter does not use the usual ‘forever’, but the effect is the same.\f* Amen.
